  narrativeContent: `Hudson Bay is an inland sea, and for the peoples who lived around it the rivers that ran into it were the roads. The Omushkego, the Swampy Cree, lived on the Severn, the Winisk, the Attawapiskat and the Albany in the flat wet lowlands of the west and south shores. The Eeyou, the Cree of the east side, lived on the Rupert, the Eastmain and La Grande. North of the trees the Dene of the Seal River and the Caribou Inuit of the Kazan, the Thelon and Baker Lake lived by the great herds. In Ungava, the Inuit of the coast and the Naskapi and Innu of the interior met at the Koksoak and the George.

## The ships come to the bay

Henry Hudson sailed into the bay in 1610 and died there. Thomas James and Luke Foxe mapped its shores in 1631. Then, in 1668, Groseilliers brought a small ship, the Nonsuch, to the mouth of the Rupert River and wintered there, and the furs the Cree traded him started a company. In 1670 Charles II gave the Hudson's Bay Company all the land whose rivers ran into the bay, which he had never seen and which belonged to other people. For the next hundred years the company built its posts at the river mouths, Rupert House, Moose Factory, Fort Albany, Fort Severn, York Factory and Churchill, and waited. The Cree came down the rivers in spring with their furs and went back up them with guns, kettles, cloth and tobacco. The French came too, overland from Quebec: Albanel by the Rupert in 1672, de Troyes and Iberville by Lake Abitibi in 1686 to capture the posts.

## Life at the posts

A post was a meeting place with rules. The Home Indians, Cree families who lived around the fort, hunted geese for it and kept watch for it; Miscomote at Albany was buried beside the Englishmen he had served. Captains like Wappisis received a coat each year as a mark of honour. But the rules could break. At Henley House in 1754 the master kept Wappisis's daughter and daughter-in-law and would not feed the men of the family, and the men killed him and the others; Joseph Isbister hanged three of them. The posts kept journals, and from those journals we know the weather, the trade, the sicknesses, and the names of people like Miscomote and Wappisis who left no writing of their own. James Isham and Andrew Graham wrote down what they saw of the Cree and the animals of the bay, and William Wales came all the way from London to watch Venus cross the sun at Churchill.

## Prophets and law

The company did not control what people believed. In 1842 the Cree prophet Abishabis used a brand-new writing, the syllabics James Evans had invented at Norway House, to draw the road to heaven, and for a year the people of the coast sang and painted books instead of hunting. He died at Severn House in 1843. On the upper Severn the Sucker people of Sandy Lake lived under their own law until 1907, when the Mounted Police arrested their headman Zhauwuno-geezhigo-gaubow, Jack Fiddler, for killing a windigo, and he died at Norway House.

## Up the rivers at last

Only in the nineteenth century did Europeans go far up the rivers. Finlayson and Erlandson crossed to Ungava in 1830 and Erlandson walked across Labrador in 1834. Robert Bell and A. P. Low of the Geological Survey mapped the rivers of the lowlands and of Labrador in the 1880s and 1890s. In 1893 the Tyrrell brothers ran the Dubawnt and the Thelon to Baker Lake and walked the frozen coast to Churchill. Travellers followed: Pike, Hanbury, Seton, and John Hornby, who starved on the Thelon in 1927 with his young cousin Edgar Christian.

## The bay today

The posts are towns now: Kuujjuaq, Chisasibi, Waskaganish, Fort Severn, Baker Lake. The James Bay dams of the 1970s changed the rivers and forced the first modern treaty in 1975. The Cree, Inuit and Dene of the bay are still there, on the rivers that were always theirs.`,
